function pz_mistakes_kitten_first_grooming_guide() {
    return [
        ['❌ Doing a full grooming session on day one', 'A kitten who is overwhelmed in her first encounter with a brush or clippers can decide grooming is scary long before she\'s even had a bad experience with pain.'],
        ['❌ Restraining a struggling kitten to "get it over with"', 'Forcing it through a fight teaches her that grooming means being trapped, which makes every future session harder, not easier.'],
        ['❌ Trimming nails without seeing the quick clearly', 'Kitten nails are small and the pink quick can be hard to see in dim light. Cutting into it hurts and makes her wary of clippers.'],
        ['❌ Only touching her paws right before a trim', 'If paw-handling only ever happens immediately before something she dislikes, she\'ll start avoiding you reaching for her feet at all.'],
        ['❌ Skipping ear and eye handling because she "doesn\'t need it yet"', 'Waiting until there is an actual problem to touch her ears or face for the first time means doing it while she is already uncomfortable and unfamiliar with the handling.'],
        ['❌ Using human nail clippers or scissors', 'These can crush or split a small kitten claw. A pair of cat-specific clippers designed for a kitten\'s thin nails is safer and easier to control.'],
    ];
}

function pz_faq_kitten_first_grooming_guide() {
    return [
        ['When should I start grooming my kitten?', 'You can start gentle handling, brushing, and pretend nail trims as soon as she settles into your home, typically around 8 weeks old. Early, low-key sessions during this socialization window tend to produce a cat who tolerates grooming well for life.'],
        ['How often should I brush a kitten?', 'A short-haired kitten usually needs brushing just once or twice a week to remove loose fur and spread natural oils. A long-haired kitten benefits from more frequent, even daily, brief brushing to prevent early matting as her adult coat starts coming in.'],
        ['Do kittens need baths?', 'Most healthy kittens groom themselves well enough that routine bathing is not necessary. A bath is reasonable if she gets into something sticky or soiled, or ahead of a show, but it should be brief, warm, and followed by a full dry to avoid chilling her.'],
        ['How do I trim a kitten\'s nails safely?', 'Hold the paw gently, press to extend the claw, and snip only the clear, sharp tip, staying well clear of the pink quick inside. Trim one or two nails per sitting at first, rewarding calm behavior, and build up to a full trim over several sessions.'],
        ['My kitten hates being brushed. What now?', 'Go back to shorter sessions with fewer strokes, and pair every touch with a treat. If she tenses, stop before she struggles rather than pushing through. Most kittens come around with patience; forcing it usually sets the dislike in deeper.'],
        ['When do kittens start needing real grooming care, not just handling practice?', 'Short-haired kittens can coast on brief weekly brushing for months. Long-haired kittens typically need real daily attention by around 4-6 months, once their adult coat starts coming in and mats can form behind the ears, under the arms, and around the belly.'],
        ['Is it okay to let my kitten play with the grooming brush?', 'Yes, letting her sniff, bat at, or sit near the brush before it touches her body is a good way to make the tool feel familiar and non-threatening rather than something that appears only when she is restrained.'],
